From baf1ef34ce5386d7ece05867c862bd4e73169744 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Homies Dev <dev@homies.local>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 06:29:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] fix: only claim a mutual match when the like was actually
 reciprocated
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Recommended.js showed the full-screen "It's a Match!" overlay on every
right-swipe:

    const person = cards.find(c => c._id === id);
    acceptUser(id);
    if (person) setMatch(person);

There was no reciprocity check anywhere in that path. A one-sided like
was presented to the student as a confirmed mutual match. The backend did
have real mutual-match logic, but only in GET /api/hostels/matches, for
the hostel admin, so the two halves of the system disagreed about what a
match is. Since mutual matching is the central claim of the project, an
examiner right-swiping a fresh profile would have been shown a match
that, by the system's own admin view, did not exist.

POST /api/profile/accept now returns { profile, mutual, matchedWith } and
the overlay fires only when `mutual` is true. Otherwise the swipe gets a
quieter confirmation that the like was recorded.

Reciprocity is decided on the server rather than in the browser. The
client could have worked it out — /recommended was returning every
candidate's full `accepted` array — but that meant shipping other
students' complete swipe history, plus their email addresses, to anyone
who loaded the feed. Those fields are now stripped from the payload and
replaced by a single `likesYou` boolean per candidate, which tells you a
match is available to you without telling you who else they have liked.
The swipe card surfaces that as a "They already liked you" hint, which is
also the strongest reason a user has to keep swiping.

Two smaller fixes ride along because they are in the same swipe path:

- Touch handlers. The card bound only onMouseDown/Move/Up, so on a phone
  the drag gesture did nothing and only the buttons worked. The README
  calls this a Tinder-style, mobile-first interface, so the gesture not
  working on mobile was the gap most likely to be noticed in a demo.

- Failed swipes are now visible. Both accept and reject dispatched a
  success alert before the request settled and swallowed errors into
  RECOMMENDATIONS_ERROR, which nothing renders. A student could pass on
  someone, see the card leave the stack, and have nothing recorded. The
  one outcome this must never produce is a user believing a swipe was
  saved when it was not.

Rejected: putting the mutual-match result into Redux. It is a transient
one-shot event consumed immediately by the component that triggered it,
so the thunk returns it directly rather than adding state that would then
need clearing.

Does not fix: no email is sent when a match becomes mutual. Both students
still only find out by opening the app.
